03. PRIORITY TWO — PIP AS HELPER AND NARRATOR
============================================================

Pip is not merely a floating decorative companion.

Pip must become one of the most important parts of the
player's experience.

Pip has THREE responsibilities:

A. COMPANION
B. ENVIRONMENTAL HELPER
C. STORY NARRATOR

A. COMPANION BEHAVIOR

Pip should:
- Follow Nima naturally.
- Hover and react to movement.
- Look toward interesting objects.
- React to dangerous situations.
- Celebrate discoveries.
- React emotionally during story moments.
- Display curiosity.
- React when Nima uses Wake.

Do not make Pip behave like a rigid navigation marker.

B. ENVIRONMENTAL HELP

Pip should help players understand nearby interactions.

Examples:

When approaching a dormant flower:
- Pip notices the flower.
- Pip flies toward it slightly.
- Its wings or body glow.
- A subtle visual cue communicates interest.

When approaching an unexplored path:
- Pip may look or hover in the direction of discovery.

When a player struggles with an environmental puzzle:
- Pip may provide a subtle hint after an appropriate delay.

Do not make Pip solve the puzzle automatically.

Do not make Pip move the player.

C. STORY NARRATOR

Implement a proper dialogue and narration system.

Pip should deliver:
- Chapter introductions.
- Short environmental observations.
- Helpful gameplay hints.
- Reactions to discoveries.
- Emotional story moments.
- Dialogue during important transitions.

Use contextual triggers.

Do not make Pip constantly speak.

Do not repeat the same dialogue every time the player
re-enters an area.

Create a data-driven dialogue system.

Suggested structure:

dialogue/
  prologue.ts
  sleepingCanopy.ts
  moonlitMarsh.ts
  hollowBelow.ts
  windwardRuins.ts

Each dialogue entry should support:

- Unique dialogue identifier.
- Trigger condition.
- Text.
- Priority.
- Optional speaker.
- Optional sound reference.
- Display duration.
- Once-per-save behavior.

VISUAL PRESENTATION:

Use elegant speech bubbles or subtle subtitle cards
integrated into the game's visual style.

Place the dialogue near Pip without obscuring Nima.

Avoid large modern chat windows.

LANGUAGE:

Use simple, accessible everyday English.

Avoid complicated scientific, astronomical, aerospace,
or overly literary terminology.

Example:

"That little flower is still asleep."

"Try sending it a Wake pulse."

"There's something moving over there."

"Careful, Nima. The path ends ahead."

"Look at those lights. They're coming back."

These are examples of tone, not mandatory dialogue.

The dialogue should feel magical, emotional and natural.

Support:
- Subtitle visibility.
- Dialogue timing.
- Skip or dismiss.
- No repeated unwanted dialogue.
- Future voice acting compatibility.

Voice acting is optional for this milestone.

Do not use runtime AI text generation.

All game dialogue must be authored and stored in the project.

