09. HYBRID 2D / 2.5D LIGHTING SYSTEM
============================================================

The current visual lighting is not sufficiently distinctive.

We want a gloomy, magical, cinematic environment.

Explore a hybrid lighting architecture that combines
2D gameplay with selected 2.5D visual techniques.

IMPORTANT TECHNICAL BOUNDARY:

We are not requiring hardware-accelerated real-time
3D ray tracing.

Do not introduce a full Three.js rendering scene merely
to imitate advanced lighting.

Do not sacrifice browser performance.

Use the existing Phaser WebGL renderer and compatible
shader or rendering techniques where practical.

Implement a 2D/2.5D lighting system.

A. DYNAMIC LIGHT SOURCES

Create localized lighting sources from:

- Nima's glowing seed.
- Pip.
- Wake pulses.
- Awakened flowers.
- Checkpoints.
- Bioluminescent plants.
- Environmental magical objects.

Lights should have adjustable radius, intensity and color.

B. DYNAMIC SHADOWS

Introduce real-time 2D shadow projection where practical.

Shadow sources:
- Large tree trunks.
- Overhanging roots.
- Branches.
- Platform formations.
- Large foreground vegetation.

Shadows should:
- Follow a consistent light direction.
- Change according to environmental lighting.
- Be soft rather than excessively sharp.
- Preserve gameplay readability.

Use geometry-based shadow shapes or appropriate shader
masking techniques.

C. NORMAL-MAPPED LIGHTING

Explore normal maps or equivalent directional lighting
maps for selected environmental artwork.

Allow light to interact with:
- Bark.
- Moss.
- Rocks.
- Leaves.
- Character materials.
- Magical objects.

Do not create an overly expensive normal map for every
tiny particle.

Prioritize the most important visible surfaces.

D. ATMOSPHERIC LIGHTING

Implement:
- Volumetric-style sunrays.
- Light shafts through trees.
- Atmospheric fog.
- Controlled lens flares.
- Directional light.
- Localized glow.
- Environmental color grading.
- Depth-aware darkness.

Light shafts should subtly change with the direction
of the environmental light.

Lens flares should be rare and restrained.

Do not make the scene constantly bloom or flash.

E. GLOOMY VISUAL IDENTITY

The world should have stronger dark-to-light separation.

Use:
- Deep forest shadows.
- Muted teal.
- Desaturated green.
- Indigo.
- Purple accents.
- Restrained warm golden light.
- Selective luminous objects.

Do not simply apply a dark overlay over the entire game.

The gloom must come from actual lighting, depth and
environmental composition.

F. LIGHTING QUALITY SETTINGS

Implement quality tiers:

- Low.
- Balanced.
- High.
- Automatic.

Adjust lighting complexity based on hardware and display.

Mobile devices should use simpler shadow and light passes.

Preserve the ability to run on desktop systems comparable
to GTX 1050 Ti with 16 GB RAM.

Do not make ray tracing a requirement.

The objective is a convincing cinematic appearance,
not a technically expensive rendering label.

